[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding .NET Garbage Collection: Generations, Heaps, and GC Modes"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Memory management in .NET is handled automatically by the Garbage Collector (GC). The GC acts as an automatic memory manager, allocating and releasing memory for your applications. Understanding how the GC organizes memory through generations, heaps, and operating modes is crucial for writing high-performance, scalable applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Generational Hypothesis"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The .NET GC is based on the generational hypothesis, which states that most objects die young. To optimize performance, the managed heap is divided into three generations: Gen 0, Gen 1, and Gen 2."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Generation 0 (Gen 0): The youngest generation, containing short-lived objects such as temporary variables. Garbage collections occur most frequently here."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Generation 1 (Gen 1): Acts as a buffer between short-lived and long-lived objects. When Gen 0 fills up, a collection runs, and surviving objects are promoted here."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Generation 2 (Gen 2): Contains long-lived objects, such as static data and global application state. Collections here are expensive and known as full GC sweeps."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Specialized Heaps: SOH, LOH, and POH"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To prevent memory fragmentation and optimize allocation patterns, .NET uses three distinct heaps:"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Small Object Heap (SOH): Where objects smaller than 85,000 bytes are allocated. This heap is subject to generational collections and compaction."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Large Object Heap (LOH): Allocated for objects >= 85,000 bytes (typically large arrays and strings). LOH objects are not compacted by default to avoid high CPU costs, which can lead to memory fragmentation."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pinned Object Heap (POH): Introduced in .NET 5, this heap allows developers to allocate objects that must not be moved by the GC (pinned objects), avoiding the performance penalties of pinning objects on the SOH."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"GC Modes: Workstation vs. Server GC"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":".NET provides different garbage collection modes depending on the workload profile of your application. You can configure these in the project file or via runtime configuration files."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Workstation GC: Optimized for client and desktop applications. It runs on the same thread that triggered the allocation and minimizes pauses."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Server GC: Optimized for high-throughput, multi-threaded server applications (such as ASP.NET Core web APIs). It dedicates a separate GC thread to each logical processor."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring GC Modes"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can explicitly configure Server GC in your application's .csproj file to optimize backend throughput."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"<PropertyGroup>
  <ServerGarbageCollection>true</ServerGarbageCollection>
  <ConcurrentGarbageCollection>true</ConcurrentGarbageCollection>
</PropertyGroup>"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Monitoring Garbage Collection in Code"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can programmatically inspect GC metrics, such as generation counts and total memory allocated, using the GC class API."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"using System;

class Program {
    static void Main() {
        long totalMemory = GC.GetTotalMemory(false);
        Console.WriteLine($"Total Allocated Memory: {totalMemory} bytes");

        Console.WriteLine($"Gen 0 Collections: {GC.CollectionCount(0)}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Gen 1 Collections: {GC.CollectionCount(1)}");
        Console.WriteLine($"Gen 2 Collections: {GC.CollectionCount(2)}");
    }
}"}]
